 ## Overview
-NeRF Rendering (Alpha Compositing) is the differentiable volume rendering technique that synthesizes novel views by tracing camera rays through a scene represented by implicit density and color functions, computing the pixel color via numerical integration of the volume rendering equation [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of mapping continuous 5D radiance fields to 2D images by approximating the line integral of emitted color weighted by accumulated transmittance [S10]. What sets it apart from explicit volume rendering (e.g., [NeRF-style Volume Rendering Integrator](5964-nerf-style-volume-rendering-integrator.md)) is its reliance on a neural network to predict density and color at arbitrary 3D points, requiring stratified sampling and alpha compositing to handle the continuous nature of the field [S1, S23].
+NeRF Rendering (Alpha Compositing) is the differentiable volume rendering technique used to synthesize novel views from a continuous 5D radiance field represented by a neural network [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of mapping discrete 3D samples of density and color along a camera ray into a final 2D pixel color and depth by numerically integrating the volume rendering equation [S1][S6, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from explicit volume rendering methods by using a continuous implicit function $F_\Theta(x, d) \rightarrow (\sigma, c)$ evaluated at arbitrary points, and from ray marching solvers by relying on stratified sampling and alpha compositing rather than surface intersection detection [S1][S19, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Camera ray $r(t) = o + td$ defined by origin $o$ and direction $d$; a trained neural network $F_\Theta$ (or separate MLPs) that maps $(x, d) \to (\sigma, c)$; sampling positions $t_i$ along the ray within bounds $[t_n, t_f]$ [S1, S10].
-*   **Outputs:** Rendered RGB color $\hat{C}(r)$ and expected depth $\hat{D}(r)$ for the pixel associated with the ray [S1][S6, unofficial].
+**Inputs:**
+*   Camera ray $r(t) = o + td$, where $o$ is the camera origin and $d$ is the unit viewing direction [S1, S4].
+*   Integration bounds $t_n$ (near) and $t_f$ (far), defining the segment of the ray to render [S1, S10].
+*   Number of samples $N$ (or $K$) [S3, S12].
+*   Neural network model $F_\Theta$ that takes 3D position $x$ and view direction $d$ and outputs volume density $\sigma$ and RGB color $c$ [S10].
+
+**Outputs:**
+*   Rendered RGB color $\hat{C}(r)$ [S1].
+*   Rendered expected depth $\hat{D}(r)$ [S1].
+*   (Optional) Accumulated opacity $\hat{\alpha}(r)$ [S6, unofficial].
 
